Relate type arguments to bounds naming other type parameters

Under capture checking, an application of `[element, coll <: Coll[element]]`
whose arguments are inferred was rejected when the collection's elements
capture something: "Type argument Coll[Box^{cap}] does not conform to upper
bound Coll[Box^{}]". Two things were wrong.

The arguments are compared with their bounds in full only in postCheck,
after capture sets are solved, and nothing before that related the capture
set inside the argument for `coll` to the one of the argument for `element`,
which was therefore solved without it. recheckTypeApply now makes that
comparison, for each inferred argument whose upper bound names a type
parameter of the same clause, while the sets can still grow; a comparison
that fails is undone and left for postCheck to report.

And postCheck put the arguments into the bounds with the box status they
have when compared with their own bounds, so the argument for `element` went
into `Coll[element]` unboxed, where an argument of an applied type is always
boxed: an argument could then not conform to a bound with the very same
capture sets. The bound is now instantiated with the arguments as they are,
and takes the bound's own box status at its top level only.

Found in Soundness, whose Traversable and Mappable instances are given for
`[element, list <: List[element]]` (Proscala issue 62).

